      "writtenOn": "2022-10-10T14:06:04Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t think it is a good idea. To use the callbacks to sensors we need to create the `device` and `pm` structures with `PM_DEVICE_DT_DEFINE` and `DEVICE_DT_DEFINE` macros. Then we need to connect the created device with the entry in the `temp_sensors` array. It kind of looks like overkill. The callback mechanism should be good once we upstream the temperature sensors to Zephyr.\n\nRegarding flash uses, it is 1328B vs 656B the previous implementation.\n\nSome Zephyr sensor drivers use the `pm_device_state_get` anyway, e.g. https://github.com/zephyrproject-rtos/zephyr/blob/main/drivers/sensor/bme280/bme280.c#L182",
      "parentUuid": "5e0d4d91_969b5602",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 332,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 332,
        "endChar": 20
      },
      "revId": "e6246bbfa6a686781f440beaa159c67505400c15",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d7715813_b374f7b6",
        "filename": "zephyr/shim/src/temp_sensors.c",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 332,
      "author": {
        "id": 1308749
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-10-11T23:35:37Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Patchset #6 better follows the upstream convention and `pm_device_state_get()` is only used by upstream drivers to fetch their own state.  However, I\u0027m okay with deferring the PM callbacks until we upstream the temperature sensor drivers.\n\nI do think you should create a separate file for the s5_domain driver.  That driver should comment that the PM callback support should be added once the temperature sensor drivers are upstream.",
      "parentUuid": "30ec8ae8_4c57c21a",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 332,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 332,
        "endChar": 20
      },
      "revId": "e6246bbfa6a686781f440beaa159c67505400c15",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5d4ecd43_a180e5a2",
        "filename": "zephyr/shim/src/temp_sensors.c",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 332,
      "author": {
        "id": 1411064
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-10-12T13:54:07Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Agree, checking the state of the power domain by devices isn\u0027t the way how it should work. \n\nI took one more high-level look at the power domain feature and I\u0027m not sure if we should use it here. Changing a PM state of power domain means that we physically power on/off devices. That\u0027s why we should use `PM_DEVICE_ACTION_TURN_ON/PM_DEVICE_ACTION_TURN_OFF` PM actions, as you mentioned above. The domain PM callback action looks like:\n```\nswitch (action):\n    case PM_DEVICE_ACTION_RESUME:\n        /* Physically power-on power, e.g. set GPIO*/ \n\n        /* Notify devices they are now powered */\n        pm_device_children_action_run(dev,  PM_DEVICE_ACTION_TURN_ON, NULL);\n    case PM_DEVICE_ACTION_SUSPEND:\n       /* Notify devices power is going down */\n        pm_device_children_action_run(dev, PM_DEVICE_ACTION_TURN_OFF, NULL);\n\n        /* Physically power-on power, e.g. clear GPIO*/ \n\n...\n```\n\nThe first problem is that the temperature sensors are still powered when the chip enters G3 state. I think it would be better to change their PM states to `PM_DEVICE_STATE_SUSPENDED`.\n\nThe second issue is that EC doesn\u0027t control the actual state of the domain, we just know its state. `pm_device_children_action_run(dev, PM_DEVICE_ACTION_TURN_OFF, NULL);` should be called before power is down to prepare devices. \n\nHowever, I don\u0027t have a good alternate proposal how to handle the issue with I2C errors in G3.",
      "parentUuid": "d7715813_b374f7b6",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 332,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 332,
        "endChar": 20
      },
      "revId": "e6246bbfa6a686781f440beaa159c67505400c15",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
